{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d6e397a4-8ef0-4e3f-aa12-0e1ab89aceab","agentId":"a1bf9e23bdb17aa09","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Repo C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers (live layer cuore/live/: transport.py, buses.py, ops.py, capture.py, poller.py, fullscan.py, uds.py, did_catalog.py; obd2-mcp/). Python .venv\\Scripts\\python.exe. No subagents; never open COM3 (the car may be connected and the main session owns the port) — analysis from code, logs and docs only; unit tests with fakes. WebFetch/WebSearch allowed (≤10 calls) for the STN1170 / vLinker FS r2 command reference (STPX, STM, ST filters, STPBR, baud, multi-frame), ISO 15765-4 timing (P2, STmin/BS), Security Gateway behaviour on 2018+ FCA (which services pass, AutoAuth), and what MES/wiTECH/AlfaOBD use on CAN-C/IHS/CH. Minimal repo grepping. Report under 150 words. Others own fullscan/liveboard/scan_health/scanlog, system_detail, dossier MIL, mes analysis/catalog/cuore_log, experience_*, mes_handoff: do not edit; you own NEW docs/research/OBD_CONNECTIVITY_REVIEW_2026-10-14.md, cuore/live/transport.py, buses.py, poller.py, uds.py (careful, additive, behaviour-preserving unless the doc says otherwise), cuore/tests/check_transport_perf.py (new). You may commit and push (`github` main only; never koch-mcp/).\n\nOwner's question: how can CUORE's OBD connectivity improve in performance and reach, and how does it compare with MES and other scan tools? Do:\n1. Review (the doc): the current path — vLinker FS r2 (STN1170 v4.3.2) over USB at 115200, port opened per operation, ATH1/ATCAF1, STP 33/34 routes, passive verify + active 0100 probe, 29-bit legislated addressing on this car, STFAC filter issue, ISO-TP reassembly, UDS 0x22/0x19 per module, poller rates, capture via STM/ATMA, cable model (none/blue A5/grey A6), Security Gateway constraints (what passed tonight: legislated OBD + UDS 19/22 on CAN-C modules; what didn't: discovery hits 0, ESM/DASM). Measure from tonight's audit log (C:\\ProgramData\\cuore\\audit.jsonl, read-only) the per-call timings: session open/close overhead, DTC read, freeze-frame per PID, UDS sweep; identify the cost centres.\n2. Compare with MES (ELM/STN adapters at up to 2 Mbit via STBR? MES recommends 115200/500000 for vLinker FS?), wiTECH/AutoAuth (gateway unlock), AlfaObd, and a native CAN interface (SocketCAN/PCAN, STN's STPX): protocol coverage (ISO 15765 11/29-bit, KWP n/a here), buses reachable, session persistence, request pipelining, multi-PID requests (Mode 01 up to 6 PIDs per request on CAN — CUORE sends one), 29-bit CAN flow-control handling, baud rate raise (STBR/STSBR to 500 kbaud or 2 Mbaud on the vLinker USB), keeping the port open across a scan (one session for the whole full scan instead of open/close per op), adaptive timing, STN's STPX for raw multi-frame, STFAP/STFPA pass filters instead of global clear, hardware alternatives (a second adapter on the blue/grey cable to avoid swapping; a PCAN/Kvaser USB-CAN for passive capture at full bus rate; a Bluetooth/WiFi vLinker for the phone).\n3. Implement the safe, measurable wins now, behaviour-preserving and tested with fakes: (a) multi-PID Mode 01 requests (up to 6 PIDs per frame per J1979) in the PID snapshot and poller, with per-ECU reply splitting; (b) one held session across a full scan / guided segment (session reuse in transport with explicit release; the per-op open stays the default elsewhere); (c) optional higher UART baud negotiated via STBR with fallback and recorded in status (off by default, CUORE_ADAPTER_BAUD setting); (d) STFAP/STCFCPA pass-filter use instead of the STP re-issue where the firmware supports it (probe-based), keeping the STP fallback; (e) ISO-TP flow-control parameters (STmin/BS) set explicitly for 29-bit targets. Each with a unit test on fake streams and a before/after estimate from the audit timings in the doc.\n4. Recommendations list with cost/benefit and what needs the car to verify (do not claim verified). Run check_transport_perf.py, check_live.py, check_active_probe.py, check_fullscan.py, check_coverage.py. Commit \"OBD connectivity review; multi-PID requests, held scan session, optional baud raise, pass filters, ISO-TP timing\" with the Co-Authored-By line, push, report hash."},"uuid":"0d30a7bd-5351-4b69-9c0c-8bb5dd77d5f8","timestamp":"2026-10-14T11:06:43.823Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
